From: Cryolitia <cryolitia@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Add named UIO discovery and the Piano Goodix fingerprint driver

Use the native signed-TA backend for enrollment, verification and scoped
Linux storage through ordinary libfprint operations. Keep hardware commands
off the main context and wait for native cancellation and cleanup before
completing an action. Stored print data contains only Linux UID and finger
coordinates; templates and authentication tokens stay in the native layer.

QTEE selects one Linux user group per operation. Identify a user's enrolled
fingers together, and try separate Linux groups only after a real negative
match. This preserves credential isolation while letting fprintd use its
normal multi-finger authentication and duplicate-enrollment checks.

The optional static dependency enables this driver when supplied by the
platform, while ordinary libfprint builds retain their existing drivers.

